```js
// Debounce: run `fn` once, `delay` ms after calls stop.
function debounce(fn, delay = 300, { leading = false } = {}) {
  let timer = null;
  return function debounced(...args) {
    const callNow = leading && timer === null;
    clearTimeout(timer);
    timer = setTimeout(() => {
      timer = null;
      if (!leading) fn.apply(this, args);
    }, delay);
    if (callNow) fn.apply(this, args);
  };
}

// Throttle: run `fn` at most once per `interval`, with a trailing call.
function throttle(fn, interval = 200) {
  let last = 0, timer = null, lastArgs = null;
  return function throttled(...args) {
    const now = Date.now();
    lastArgs = args;
    if (now - last >= interval) {
      last = now;
      fn.apply(this, args);
    } else if (timer === null) {
      timer = setTimeout(() => {
        last = Date.now();
        timer = null;
        fn.apply(this, lastArgs);      // trailing edge
      }, interval - (now - last));
    }
  };
}
```

The details that separate a passing answer from a good one:

- **`fn.apply(this, args)`**, not `fn()` — otherwise `this` and the arguments are lost, which breaks method usage.
- **The trailing call in throttle.** Without it the last event in a burst is dropped, so a scroll handler ends up showing a stale position.
- **The `leading` option in debounce.** Sometimes you want the first call immediate and the rest suppressed — a submit button, for instance.
- **A `cancel()` method** in production code, so a React component can clear the pending timer on unmount and avoid setting state after teardown.

For anything that updates the screen, a time-based throttle is the wrong tool — you want exactly **one execution per frame**, aligned with when the browser is about to paint.

```js
function rafThrottle(fn) {
  let queued = false, lastArgs;
  return function (...args) {
    lastArgs = args;
    if (queued) return;              // already scheduled for this frame
    queued = true;
    requestAnimationFrame(() => {
      queued = false;
      fn.apply(this, lastArgs);
    });
  };
}

// A scroll handler that updates a progress bar
const onScroll = rafThrottle(() => {
  const pct = window.scrollY / (document.body.scrollHeight - innerHeight);
  bar.style.transform = `scaleX(${pct})`;   // composite-only update
});

window.addEventListener('scroll', onScroll, { passive: true });
```

Why this beats `throttle(fn, 16)`:

- **It's aligned with the browser's frame schedule.** A 16ms timer drifts against vsync, so you get occasional double-runs and occasional skipped frames.
- **It adapts automatically.** On a 120Hz display it runs 120 times a second; on a struggling device it runs less often instead of piling up callbacks.
- **It pauses in background tabs**, where rAF is throttled or halted — a timer keeps burning CPU and battery.

The `{ passive: true }` on the listener is a separate but essential detail: it promises the handler won't call `preventDefault()`, so the browser can start scrolling immediately instead of waiting to see. On touch devices that alone can be the difference between smooth and sticky scrolling.

```js
// worker.js — no DOM access, pure computation
self.onmessage = (e) => {
  const { rows, query } = e.data;
  // A few hundred ms of work: filtering and scoring 200k records
  const scored = rows
    .map(r => ({ r, score: rank(r, query) }))
    .filter(x => x.score > 0)
    .sort((a, b) => b.score - a.score)
    .slice(0, 50);
  self.postMessage(scored);
};
```

```js
// main.js
const worker = new Worker(new URL('./worker.js', import.meta.url),
                          { type: 'module' });

let pending = 0;
function search(query) {
  const id = ++pending;
  showSpinner();                      // UI stays fully interactive meanwhile
  worker.postMessage({ rows, query });
  worker.onmessage = (e) => {
    if (id !== pending) return;       // drop results from a stale query
    render(e.data);
  };
}

// Moving binary data with zero copy
const buf = new ArrayBuffer(64 * 1024 * 1024);
worker.postMessage(buf, [buf]);       // transferred, not cloned
```

Three production details this sketch shows:

- **Stale-result guarding.** Workers are asynchronous, so responses can arrive out of order. Tag each request and discard replies that aren't the latest — otherwise a slow early query overwrites a fast later one.
- **Reuse the worker.** Creating one per request throws away the startup cost every time; keep a long-lived worker, or a small pool for parallelism.
- **Send data once.** If `rows` is large, sending it with every keystroke re-clones megabytes on the main thread. Send it once at init and keep it inside the worker.

Libraries like `comlink` wrap all of this in a promise-based RPC interface, which removes most of the boilerplate without hiding the cost model.

When work must touch the DOM, a worker is out — so the answer is to split the task and hand control back between pieces.

```js
// Modern: scheduler.yield() resumes with high priority, so your work
// continues promptly after the browser handles pending input.
function yieldToMain() {
  if ('scheduler' in window && 'yield' in scheduler) return scheduler.yield();
  return new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, 0));
}

async function processAll(items) {
  let start = performance.now();
  for (const item of items) {
    process(item);
    if (performance.now() - start > 40) {   // stay under the 50ms line
      await yieldToMain();
      start = performance.now();
    }
  }
}

// Non-urgent work, run only when the browser is idle
requestIdleCallback((deadline) => {
  while (deadline.timeRemaining() > 0 && queue.length) {
    sendAnalytics(queue.pop());
  }
}, { timeout: 2000 });
```

The **time-based** check is the important part — yielding every N items is wrong, because item cost varies. Measuring elapsed time and yielding at ~40ms keeps every chunk under the long-task threshold regardless of what the items are.

The three schedulers, and when each is right:

- **`scheduler.yield()`** — for work that must finish soon. It resumes at high priority, so you don't lose your place behind other queued tasks.
- **`setTimeout(0)`** — the universal fallback. Works everywhere, but resumes at normal task priority, so long chunks of unrelated work can jump ahead.
- **`requestIdleCallback`** — for genuinely optional work: analytics flushes, prefetching, cache warming. Always pass a `timeout`, or on a busy page it may never run.

All of this is worthless applied to the wrong function, so the diagnostic step comes first.

**In development:** record the interaction in the Performance panel, find tasks with a red corner triangle, and descend the flame chart to the widest child until you reach your own code. The Interactions track shows how long the user's input waited, which distinguishes an input-delay problem (something else was running) from a processing problem (your handler is slow).

**In production:** a `PerformanceObserver` on `longtask` reports every task over 50ms with a rough attribution. Wired into RUM, it tells you which routes have long tasks on real devices — where they actually happen, since a 400ms task on your laptop may be 2 seconds on a mid-tier phone.

```js
new PerformanceObserver(list => {
  for (const t of list.getEntries()) {
    if (t.duration > 100) report('longtask', t.duration, location.pathname);
  }
}).observe({ type: 'longtask', buffered: true });
```

The judgement to apply to the result:

- **Under 50ms?** Leave it alone. Debouncing a 5ms handler adds complexity and bugs for nothing.
- **50-200ms, triggered by a stream of events?** Throttle or debounce.
- **Over 200ms, one-off, no DOM?** Worker.
- **Over 200ms and DOM-bound?** Chunk it, or make it smaller.

And always throttle the CPU 4-6x when testing. A handler that's fine on your machine and terrible on a real phone is the single most common way this goes unnoticed until users complain.
